{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.10"}, "document": {"page_count": 2145, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 205, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 206, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 207, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 208, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 209, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 210, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 211, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 212, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 213, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 214, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 215, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 216, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 217, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 205, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.144, 0.072, 0.856, 0.25], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.144, 0.072, 0.856, 0.25], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.142, 0.263, 0.857, 0.441], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.142, 0.263, 0.857, 0.441], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.143, 0.456, 0.857, 0.676], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.143, 0.456, 0.857, 0.676], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.685, 0.855, 0.812], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Stages in an RBFS search for the shortest route to Bucharest. The -limit value for each recursive call isf shown on top of each current node, and every node is labeled with its -cost. (a) The path via Rimnicuf Vilcea is followed until the current best leaf (Pitesti) has a value that is worse than the best alternative path (Fagaras). (b) The recursion unwinds and the best leaf value of the forgotten subtree (417) is backed up to Rimnicu Vilcea; then Fagaras is expanded, revealing a best leaf value of 450. (c) The recursion unwinds and the best leaf value of the forgotten subtree (450) is backed up to Fagaras; then Rimnicu Vilcea is expanded. This time, because the best alternative path (through Timisoara) costs at least 447, the expansion continues to Bucharest."}]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.879, 0.408, 0.896], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Recursive best-first search"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 206, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.217, 0.092, 0.345, 0.109], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Backed-up value"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.175, 0.857, 0.36], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "RBFS is somewhat more efficient than", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { I D A ^ { * } } ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "but still suffers from excessive node regeneration. In the example in Figure 3.23 , RBFS follows the path via Rimnicu Vilcea,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "then “changes its mind” and tries Fagaras, and then changes its mind back again. These mind changes occur because every time the current best path is extended, its -value is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "likely to increase— is usually less optimistic for nodes closer to a goal. When this happens,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h "}, {"type": "text", "content": "the second-best path might become the best path, so the search has to backtrack to follow it. Each mind change corresponds to an iteration of", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { I D A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and could require many reexpansions of forgotten nodes to recreate the best path and extend it one more node.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.389, 0.852, 0.502], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "RBFS is optimal if the heuristic function", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is admissible. Its space complexity is linear in the depth of the deepest optimal solution, but its time complexity is rather difficult to characterize: it depends both on the accuracy of the heuristic function and on how often the best path changes as nodes are expanded. It expands nodes in order of increasing -score,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "even if is nonmonotonic.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.531, 0.845, 0.645], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "IDA* and RBFS suffer from using too little memory. Between iterations,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { I D A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "retains only a single number: the current -cost limit. RBFS retains more information in memory, but it", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "uses only linear space: even if more memory were available, RBFS has no way to make use of it. Because they forget most of what they have done, both algorithms may end up reexploring the same states many times over.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.674, 0.858, 0.907], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "It seems sensible, therefore, to determine how much memory we have available, and allow an algorithm to use all of it. Two algorithms that do this are", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { M A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(memory-bounded A*)", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " and "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathbf { S M A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " (simplified "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { M A ^ { * } ) }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { S M A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " is—well—simpler, so we will describe it. "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { S M A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "proceeds just like A*, expanding the best leaf until memory is full. At this point, it cannot add a new node to the search tree without dropping an old one. SMA* always drops the worst leaf node—the one with the highest -value. Like RBFS, SMA* then backs up the value", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "of the forgotten node to its parent. In this way, the ancestor of a forgotten subtree knows the quality of the best path in that subtree. With this information,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { S M A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "regenerates the subtree only when all other paths have been shown to look worse than the path it has forgotten. Another way of saying this is that if all the descendants of a node are forgotten, then we", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 207, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.851, 0.12], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "will not know which way to go from but we will still have an idea of how worthwhile it is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n, "}, {"type": "text", "content": "to go anywhere from", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " n."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.217, 0.187, 0.261, 0.203], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "MA*", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.284, 0.271, 0.299], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "SMA*", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.367, 0.854, 0.552], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The complete algorithm is described in the online code repository accompanying this book. There is one subtlety worth mentioning. We said that SMA* expands the best leaf and deletes the worst leaf. What if all the leaf nodes have the same -value? To avoid selecting", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "the same node for deletion and expansion, SMA* expands the newest best leaf and deletes the oldest worst leaf. These coincide when there is only one leaf, but in that case, the current search tree must be a single path from root to leaf that fills all of memory. If the leaf is not a goal node, then even if it is on an optimal solution path, that solution is not reachable with the available memory. Therefore, the node can be discarded exactly as if it had no successors.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.58, 0.852, 0.717], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "SMA* is complete if there is any reachable solution—that is, if the depth of the shallowest", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "d "}, {"type": "text", "content": "goal node, is less than the memory size (expressed in nodes). It is optimal if any optimal solution is reachable; otherwise, it returns the best reachable solution. In practical terms, SMA* is a fairly robust choice for finding optimal solutions, particularly when the state space is a graph, action costs are not uniform, and node generation is expensive compared to the overhead of maintaining the frontier and the reached set.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.746, 0.851, 0.908], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "On very hard problems, however, it will often be the case that SMA* is forced to switch back and forth continually among many candidate solution paths, only a small subset of which can fit in memory. (This resembles the problem of thrashing in disk paging systems.) Then the extra time required for repeated regeneration of the same nodes means that problems that would be practically solvable by A*, given unlimited memory, become intractable for SMA*. That is to say, memory limitations can make a problem intractable from the point of view of computation time. Although no current theory explains the tradeoff", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 208, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.857, 0.121], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "between time and memory, it seems that this is an inescapable problem. The only way out is to drop the optimality requirement.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.218, 0.187, 0.301, 0.204], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Thrashing"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.14, 0.268, 0.526, 0.288], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3.5.6 Bidirectional heuristic search"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.302, 0.852, 0.369], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "With unidirectional best-first search, we saw that using", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f ( n ) = g ( n ) + h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "as the evaluation function gives us an", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "A ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search that is guaranteed to find optimal-cost solutions (assuming an admissible ) while being optimally efficient in the number of nodes expanded.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.397, 0.857, 0.535], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "With bidirectional best-first search we could also try using", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f ( n ) = g ( n ) + h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "but", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", "}, {"type": "text", "content": "unfortunately there is no guarantee that this would lead to an optimal-cost solution, nor that it would be optimally efficient, even with an admissible heuristic. With bidirectional search, it turns out that it is not individual nodes but rather pairs of nodes (one from each frontier) that can be proved to be surely expanded, so any proof of efficiency will have to consider pairs of nodes (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A67", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Eckerle et al., 2017", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.563, 0.856, 0.701], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We’ll start with some new notation. We use", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { F } ( n ) = g _ { F } ( n ) + h _ { F } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "for nodes going in the forward direction (with the initial state as root) and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { B } ( n ) = g _ { B } ( n ) + h _ { B } ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "for nodes in the backward direction (with a goal state as root). Although both forward and backward searches are solving the same problem, they have different evaluation functions because, for example, the heuristics are different depending on whether you are striving for the goal or for the initial state. We’ll assume admissible heuristics.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.73, 0.855, 0.796], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Consider a forward path from the initial state to a node and a backward path from the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "m "}, {"type": "text", "content": "goal to a node We can define a lower bound on the cost of a solution that follows the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "n. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "path from the initial state to", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "m ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "then somehow gets to then follows the path to the goal as", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " n"}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.319, 0.819, 0.678, 0.837], "content": "l b (m, n) = \\max \\left(g _ {F} (m) + g _ {B} (n), f _ {F} (m), f _ {B} (n)\\right)"}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.139, 0.864, 0.846, 0.906], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In other words, the cost of such a path must be at least as large as the sum of the path costs of the two parts (because the remaining connection between them must have nonnegative", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 209, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.848, 0.334], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "cost), and the cost must also be at least as much as the estimated", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "cost of either part (because the heuristic estimates are optimistic). Given that, the theorem is that for any pair of nodes with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "m, n "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "l b ( m , n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "less than the optimal cost", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "we must expand either or", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", m "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "n ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "because the path that goes through both of them is a potential optimal solution. The difficulty is that we don’t know for sure which node is best to expand, and therefore no bidirectional search algorithm can be guaranteed to be optimally efficient—any algorithm might expand up to twice the minimum number of nodes if it always chooses the wrong member of a pair to expand first. Some bidirectional heuristic search algorithms explicitly", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F254.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F2FA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "manage a queue of", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "( m , n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F254.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F2FA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "pairs, but we will stick with bidirectional best-first search (Figure", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "3 . 1 4 \\boxdot \\triangle )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F254.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F2FA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ", which has two frontier priority queues, and give it an evaluation function that", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "mimics the criteria:", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "lb"}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "equation", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.374, 0.358, 0.624, 0.376], "content": "f _ {2} (n) = \\max (2 g (n), g (n) + h (n))"}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.403, 0.854, 0.517], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The node to expand next will be the one that minimizes this", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "value; the node can come from either frontier. This", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "function guarantees that we will never expand a node (from either frontier) with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { g ( n ) > \\frac { C ^ { * } } { 2 } } \\end{array}"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We say the two halves of the search “meet in the middle” in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": ". "}, {"type": "text", "content": "the sense that when the two frontiers touch, no node inside of either frontier has a path cost greater than the bound", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\frac { C ^ { * } } { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.24 works through an example bidirectional search.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "."}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.266, 0.579, 0.735, 0.764], "content": [{"type": "image_caption", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.141, 0.546, 0.233, 0.563], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.24"}]}, {"type": "image_body", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.266, 0.579, 0.735, 0.764], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.775, 0.854, 0.921], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bidirectional search maintains two frontiers: on the left, nodes A and B are successors of Start; on the right, node F is an inverse successor of Goal. Each node is labeled with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f = g + h"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "values and the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { 2 } = \\operatorname* { m a x } ( 2 g , g + h )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "value. (The", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "g"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "values are the sum of the action costs as shown on each arrow; the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "  h "}, {"type": "text", "content": "values are arbitrary and cannot be derived from anything in the figure.) The optimal solution, Start-A-F-Goal, has cost", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "C ^ { * }   =   4 + 2 + 4   =   1 0 ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "so that means that a meet-in-the-middle bidirectional algorithm should not expand any node with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\begin{array} { r } { g > \\frac { C ^ { * } } { 2 }   =   5 ; } \\end{array}"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and indeed the next node to be expanded would be A or F (each with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "g   =   4 )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ",", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " leading us to an optimal solution. If we expanded the node with lowest cost first,f "}, {"type": "text", "content": "then B and C would come next, and D and E would be tied with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "but they all have", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "g > \\frac { C ^ { * } } { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and thus are never expanded when", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is the evaluation function.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 210, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.219, 0.143, 0.318, 0.159], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Front-to-end", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.218, 0.239, 0.328, 0.256], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Front-to-front"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.322, 0.856, 0.531], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have described an approach where the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { F }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "heuristic estimates the distance to the goal (or, when the problem has multiple goal states, the distance to the closest goal) and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { B }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "estimates the distance to the start. This is called a front-to-end search. An alternative, called front-to-front search, attempts to estimate the distance to the other frontier. Clearly, if a frontier has millions of nodes, it would be inefficient to apply the heuristic function to every one of them and take the minimum. But it can work to sample a few nodes from the frontier. In certain specific problem domains it is possible to summarize the frontier—for example, in a grid search problem, we can incrementally compute a bounding box of the frontier, and use as a heuristic the distance to the bounding box.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.56, 0.855, 0.745], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bidirectional search is sometimes more efficient than unidirectional search, sometimes not. In general, if we have a very good heuristic, then", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "search produces search contours that are focused on the goal, and adding bidirectional search does not help much. With an average heuristic, bidirectional search that meets in the middle tends to expand fewer nodes and is preferred. In the worst case of a poor heuristic, the search is no longer focused on the goal, and bidirectional search has the same asymptotic complexity as", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\boldsymbol { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}