 and that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is better. How might one have come up with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 } ?"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Is it possible for a computer to invent such a heuristic mechanically?", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.563, 0.854, 0.75], "content": [{"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 1 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "are estimates of the remaining path length for the 8-puzzle, but they are also perfectly accurate path lengths for simplified versions of the puzzle. If the rules of the puzzle were changed so that a tile could move anywhere instead of just to the adjacent empty square, then", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 1 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "would give the exact length of the shortest solution. Similarly, if a tile could move one square in any direction, even onto an occupied square, then", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "would give the exact length of the shortest solution. A problem with fewer restrictions on the actions is called a relaxed problem. The state-space graph of the relaxed problem is a supergraph of the original state space because the removal of restrictions creates added edges in the graph.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.218, 0.815, 0.342, 0.831], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Relaxed problem"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 215, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.08, 0.852, 0.216], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Because the relaxed problem adds edges to the state-space graph, any optimal solution in the original problem is, by definition, also a solution in the relaxed problem; but the relaxed problem may have better solutions if the added edges provide shortcuts. Hence, the cost of an optimal solution to a relaxed problem is an admissible heuristic for the original problem. Furthermore, because the derived heuristic is an exact cost for the relaxed problem, it must obey the triangle inequality and is therefore consistent (see page", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F32C.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F38D", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "88", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.14, 0.245, 0.814, 0.286], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "If a problem definition is written down in a formal language, it is possible to construct relaxed problems automatically. For example, if the 8-puzzle actions are described as14", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.14, 0.315, 0.855, 0.345], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "14 In", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001039E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001039E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 8", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "11", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", we describe formal languages suitable for this task; with formal descriptions that can be manipulated, the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001039E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001039E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "construction of relaxed problems can be automated. For now, we use English.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.338, 0.361, 0.662, 0.396], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A tile can move from square X to square Y if X is adjacent to Y and Y is blank"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.141, 0.425, 0.789, 0.537], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "we can generate three relaxed problems by removing one or both of the conditions: a. A tile can move from square X to square Y if X is adjacent to Y. b. A tile can move from square X to square Y if Y is blank. c. A tile can move from square X to square Y.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.567, 0.854, 0.751], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "From (a), we can derive", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(Manhattan distance). The reasoning is that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 2 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "would be the proper score if we moved each tile in turn to its destination. The heuristic derived from (b) is discussed in Exercise", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "3 . \\tt G A S C ."}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "From (c), we can derive", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 1 }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(misplaced tiles) because it would be the proper score if tiles could move to their intended destination in one action. Notice that it is crucial that the relaxed problems generated by this technique can be solved essentially without search, because the relaxed rules allow the problem to be decomposed into eight independent subproblems. If the relaxed problem is hard to solve, then the values of the corresponding heuristic will be expensive to obtain.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.781, 0.855, 0.869], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A program called ABSOLVER can generate heuristics automatically from problem definitions, using the “relaxed problem” method and various other techniques (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001638C", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Prieditis, 1993", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). ABSOLVER generated a new heuristic for the 8-puzzle that was better than any preexisting heuristic and found the first useful heuristic for the famous Rubik’s Cube puzzle.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 216, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.078, 0.842, 0.145], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "If a collection of admissible heuristics", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { 1 } \\ldots h _ { m }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is available for a problem and none of them is clearly better than the others, which should we choose? As it turns out, we can have the best of all worlds, by defining", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "equation", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.379, 0.168, 0.618, 0.187], "content": "h (n) = \\max \\{h _ {1} (n), \\dots , h _ {k} (n) \\}."}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.211, 0.855, 0.376], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This composite heuristic picks whichever function is most accurate on the node in question. Because the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { i }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "components are admissible, is admissible (and if", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { i }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "are all consistent, is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h "}, {"type": "text", "content": "consistent). Furthermore, dominates all of its component heuristics. The only drawback is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "h "}, {"type": "text", "content": "that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h ( n )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "takes longer to compute. If that is an issue, an alternative is to randomly select one of the heuristics at each evaluation, or use a machine learning algorithm to predict which heuristic will be best. Doing this can result in a heuristic that is inconsistent (even if every", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { i }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "is consistent), but in practice it usually leads to faster problem solving.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.14, 0.4, 0.745, 0.445], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3.6.3 Generating heuristics from subproblems: Pattern databases"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.217, 0.51, 0.311, 0.528], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Subproblem"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.594, 0.849, 0.731], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Admissible heuristics can also be derived from the solution cost of a subproblem of a given problem. For example, Figure 3.27 shows a subproblem of the 8-puzzle instance in Figure", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "3.25 . The subproblem involves getting tiles 1, 2, 3, 4, and the blank into their correct", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "positions. Clearly, the cost of the optimal solution of this subproblem is a lower bound on the cost of the complete problem. It turns out to be more accurate than Manhattan distance in some cases.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.142, 0.76, 0.232, 0.778], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.27"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 217, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.265, 0.075, 0.47, 0.258], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.265, 0.075, 0.47, 0.258], "content": ""}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.529, 0.075, 0.732, 0.234], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.529, 0.075, 0.732, 0.234], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.584, 0.241, 0.679, 0.258], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Goal State"}]}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.141, 0.268, 0.826, 0.3], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A subproblem of the 8-puzzle instance given in Figure 3.25 . The task is to get tiles 1, 2, 3, 4, and the blank into their correct positions, without worrying about what happens to the other tiles."}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.331, 0.848, 0.565], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The idea behind pattern databases is to store these exact solution costs for every possible subproblem instance—in our example, every possible configuration of the four tiles and the blank. (There will be", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "9 \\times 8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5   =   1 5 { , } 1 2 0"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "patterns in the database. The identities of the other four tiles are irrelevant for the purposes of solving the subproblem, but moves of those tiles do count toward the solution cost of the subproblem.) Then we compute an admissible heuristic", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "h _ { D B }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "for each state encountered during a search simply by looking up the corresponding subproblem configuration in the database. The database itself is constructed by searching back from the goal and recording the cost of each new pattern encountered; the expense of this search is amortized over subsequent problem instances,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "15 "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and so makes sense if we expect to be asked to solve many problems.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.141, 0.591, 0.825, 0.623], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "15 By working backward from the goal, the exact solution cost of every instance encountered is immediately available. This is an example of dynamic programming, which we discuss further in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 17", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.218, 0.687, 0.345, 0.702], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pattern database", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.139, 0.77, 0.852, 0.884], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The choice of tiles 1-2-3-4 to go with the blank is fairly arbitrary; we could also construct databases for 5-6-7-8, for 2-4-6-8, and so on. Each database yields an admissible heuristic, and these heuristics can be combined, as explained earlier, by taking the maximum value. A combined heuristic of this kind is much more accurate than the Manhattan distance; the number of nodes generated when solving random 15-puzzles can be reduced by a factor of", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}], "is_full_document": false, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}